1
00:00:07,046 --> 00:00:08,046
It's

2
00:00:23,896 --> 00:00:26,676
My pleasure to introduce Tao Pang.

3
00:00:27,016 --> 00:00:30,256
Tao Pang is a robotics
researcher at Multiply Labs,

4
00:00:30,296 --> 00:00:34,856
where he's working
on automating drug manufacturing

5
00:00:34,896 --> 00:00:38,836
using dexterous manipulation learning,

6
00:00:39,936 --> 00:00:43,036
Especially skills from egocentric videos.

7
00:00:43,076 --> 00:00:46,896
More broadly, he's interested
in enabling robots to interact

8
00:00:46,936 --> 00:00:52,196
with the world intelligently,
dexterously, and efficiently.

9
00:00:53,476 --> 00:00:55,056
So before Multiply Labs,

10
00:00:56,476 --> 00:01:01,256
Tao Pang has led the dexterous
manipulation effort as the RAI Institute.

11
00:01:02,956 --> 00:01:06,096
So with that, let's invite Tao Pang.

12
00:01:06,296 --> 00:01:07,296
Thank you.

13
00:01:07,716 --> 00:01:10,076
It's a great pleasure to be here.

14
00:01:10,816 --> 00:01:12,776
And it's also a great honor to be speaking

15
00:01:12,816 --> 00:01:16,136
right after Professor Todorov,
because we have,

16
00:01:17,076 --> 00:01:21,636
My collaborators and myself have read
the original three MuJoCo papers,

17
00:01:21,676 --> 00:01:24,616
two on dynamics formulation and one
on inverse dynamics many times,

18
00:01:24,656 --> 00:01:26,576
countless number of times.

19
00:01:26,776 --> 00:01:28,996
So I don't think I would
be able to personally give

20
00:01:29,036 --> 00:01:32,716
this presentation without
Professor Todorov's work.

21
00:01:33,316 --> 00:01:34,316
All right.

22
00:01:34,876 --> 00:01:38,716
So the organizer today gave
me a list of challenges

23
00:01:38,756 --> 00:01:41,136
and asked me to talk about some of them.

24
00:01:41,556 --> 00:01:45,816
But I think even burning a burning
question that every roboticist has been

25
00:01:45,856 --> 00:01:49,896
asking themselves lately is,
is the next release of Astra or Fable

26
00:01:49,936 --> 00:01:53,176
going to solve all these problems
and we will be out of our jobs.

27
00:01:54,146 --> 00:01:56,676
I think before trying
to answer that question,

28
00:01:56,716 --> 00:02:02,056
maybe let's take a closer look at how
agentic systems is solving robotics tasks.

29
00:02:02,556 --> 00:02:04,316
So this paper Rex came out last week,

30
00:02:04,356 --> 00:02:09,996
and they did a bunch of benchmarking
of agentic systems with different backend

31
00:02:10,036 --> 00:02:14,816
models on a variety
of common robot benchmarks,

32
00:02:14,856 --> 00:02:17,576
like robot outro, text outro, etc.

33
00:02:17,616 --> 00:02:23,716
And in none of these tasks does
their agents do the agents not use tools.

34
00:02:24,656 --> 00:02:26,136
So for example, they would
use forward kinematics,

35
00:02:26,176 --> 00:02:29,636
they would use inverse kinematics,
they would do collision detection,

36
00:02:29,676 --> 00:02:32,416
and sometimes they even do
collision free motion planning.

37
00:02:33,176 --> 00:02:34,176
So,

38
00:02:34,376 --> 00:02:39,556
So in today's talk, I'm going to argue
that if we can understand some

39
00:02:39,596 --> 00:02:44,156
of the harnesses behind these challenges,

40
00:02:44,196 --> 00:02:47,236
we could be building better
tools for these agents so

41
00:02:47,276 --> 00:02:49,556
that they can solve their task
a lot more efficiently.

42
00:02:51,096 --> 00:02:53,716
Okay, since not a lot
of people have done studies

43
00:02:53,756 --> 00:02:57,876
on how tool use changes
agents' performance.

44
00:02:58,556 --> 00:03:00,316
So I'm going to propose a fairly simple

45
00:03:00,356 --> 00:03:03,756
experiment that tries
to do a small part of that.

46
00:03:03,796 --> 00:03:10,996
So here, it's me on the left, questioning
myself worse after Opus 5.5 came

47
00:03:11,036 --> 00:03:15,196
out a few weeks after Fable, which does
even better on some robotics tasks.

48
00:03:16,556 --> 00:03:21,716
And here's the task itself that I'm
asking Opus 5.5 to handle for me.

49
00:03:23,456 --> 00:03:24,456
What's that?

50
00:03:26,576 --> 00:03:29,016
Okay, so the task is about
in hand orientation

51
00:03:29,056 --> 00:03:32,996
of like of an object
with an interesting shape.

52
00:03:33,096 --> 00:03:34,676
So we have to do some
convex decomposition,

53
00:03:34,716 --> 00:03:38,416
it has some meshes, so it's
not just geometric primitives.

54
00:03:39,296 --> 00:03:42,836
And we sample random 200
random goals that are less

55
00:03:42,876 --> 00:03:46,096
than 60 degrees away from
the initial configuration.

56
00:03:46,776 --> 00:03:49,236
I think we can relax these
constraints with more tools.

57
00:03:49,396 --> 00:03:52,416
But since here we want to do an A/B
study on just one particular tool,

58
00:03:52,456 --> 00:03:54,876
we are going to keep this around.

59
00:03:54,916 --> 00:03:58,636
So we, we give the agent 50
of those goals for training.

60
00:03:58,876 --> 00:04:00,576
So which means it can look
at how to solve those goals,

61
00:04:00,616 --> 00:04:02,996
while it's trying
to figure out a strategy,

62
00:04:03,036 --> 00:04:08,396
and we keep 150 away from the agent
for evaluation or validation.

63
00:04:08,996 --> 00:04:12,076
And the purpose of this experiment
is for the agent

64
00:04:12,116 --> 00:04:15,836
to write a piece of code
that will be used as the policy.

65
00:04:16,116 --> 00:04:19,996
So when we evaluate the policy,
we no longer have the agent in the loop.

66
00:04:20,656 --> 00:04:23,516
And we want to emulate a zero
shot, a few shots scenario,

67
00:04:23,556 --> 00:04:25,796
which means we are going
to give the agents

68
00:04:25,836 --> 00:04:29,536
a limited amount of interaction
with the environment.

69
00:04:30,016 --> 00:04:31,636
And we are going to use high fidelity

70
00:04:31,676 --> 00:04:36,456
simulation for both
evaluation and exploration.

71
00:04:36,956 --> 00:04:38,136
So here we're using Drake.

72
00:04:38,376 --> 00:04:41,616
Well, you could say Drake is as close of a
simulator as we can find to a real robot,

73
00:04:41,656 --> 00:04:44,036
because it's so hard to set up.

74
00:04:47,516 --> 00:04:48,636
Anyway, okay.

75
00:04:49,236 --> 00:04:50,336
So here's the task.

76
00:04:50,656 --> 00:04:55,476
Well, I give the agent a task, and I'm
going to give the agent some resources.

77
00:04:55,736 --> 00:04:58,776
So I give each agent $10 to buy tokens,

78
00:04:58,816 --> 00:05:04,596
and I give them some tools,
for example, fingertip tools, IK tools,

79
00:05:05,876 --> 00:05:10,936
Like prediction, predict the future
after stepping some of the fingertips.

80
00:05:11,556 --> 00:05:14,736
And I also give the agent
a fairly simple prompt,

81
00:05:14,776 --> 00:05:17,356
which is take the tools and do the task.

82
00:05:19,316 --> 00:05:21,276
And this is what happened.

83
00:05:21,896 --> 00:05:25,376
Here I'm plotting the training
loss on the vertical axis.

84
00:05:25,596 --> 00:05:27,796
So there are two metrics we care about.

85
00:05:28,056 --> 00:05:32,536
One is the median final error,
and the other is the tail.

86
00:05:32,576 --> 00:05:37,436
It's the average of the last
10% of the tracking errors.

87
00:05:38,216 --> 00:05:42,236
And we run each experiment five
times so that whatever conclusion

88
00:05:42,276 --> 00:05:47,016
we draw has a small amount
of statistical significance.

89
00:05:47,896 --> 00:05:51,256
So the band here shows
the extent over the five rounds,

90
00:05:51,296 --> 00:05:53,356
and each dot here represents the round.

91
00:05:53,716 --> 00:05:56,536
And the line in the middle
represents the median.

92
00:05:56,576 --> 00:06:01,436
And on the horizontal axis, I'm plotting
how much money the agent has spent.

93
00:06:02,156 --> 00:06:04,456
And it's interesting
that sometimes the agent

94
00:06:04,496 --> 00:06:07,096
decides to stop because
it thinks it's stuck.

95
00:06:07,516 --> 00:06:10,576
So there's a harness that's
prompting the agent to continue,

96
00:06:10,616 --> 00:06:14,076
but if the agent keeps saying, oh,
I think I've tried anything I could,

97
00:06:14,116 --> 00:06:15,736
but I can't make any more progress,

98
00:06:15,776 --> 00:06:18,416
I'm going to send you some money,
three times in a row it's going to

99
00:06:18,456 --> 00:06:18,656
Stop.

100
00:06:18,696 --> 00:06:19,876
And it indeed happened.

101
00:06:20,036 --> 00:06:22,396
I think now the agents have spent $10.

102
00:06:23,776 --> 00:06:29,256
So they all decide to stop
somewhere between $9 and $7.

103
00:06:31,616 --> 00:06:36,496
But what if I tell you that with
the addition of one simple tool,

104
00:06:36,536 --> 00:06:42,356
I can get a very different looking
curve on the training loss?

105
00:06:43,716 --> 00:06:46,916
So especially if we look
at the $2 mark here,

106
00:06:46,956 --> 00:06:52,256
this other agent starts off
with a much better average.

107
00:06:52,676 --> 00:06:55,496
But if you even look at
this worst case, the tail plot,

108
00:06:55,536 --> 00:07:00,736
this other agent is able
to converge with just $2.

109
00:07:01,176 --> 00:07:04,436
And it doesn't really make
a lot of improvement after that.

110
00:07:04,476 --> 00:07:09,436
And this tool, well not
surprisingly, is MPC.

111
00:07:10,536 --> 00:07:12,716
And even with locally linear dynamics.

112
00:07:13,496 --> 00:07:17,656
So yeah, we give this linear MPC
tool to the agent and ask it to use

113
00:07:17,696 --> 00:07:21,676
it by tuning Q and R matrices a little
bit and a few more parameters.

114
00:07:22,236 --> 00:07:27,356
And it's able to do a lot better, both in
terms of median performance and the tail.

115
00:07:28,556 --> 00:07:34,316
And the validation results on the 150
unseen goals also support that statement.

116
00:07:35,576 --> 00:07:42,756
Yeah, here's the violin plot for the
tracking error of the best policy we get

117
00:07:42,796 --> 00:07:46,256
from the regular agent and the best policy

118
00:07:46,296 --> 00:07:49,196
we get from the agent
with the linear controller.

119
00:07:49,736 --> 00:07:52,136
And we can see that the agent
with the linear

120
00:07:52,176 --> 00:07:54,596
controller does a lot
better in terms of tail.

121
00:07:54,636 --> 00:07:59,816
And because we also ran
the agents multiple times,

122
00:07:59,856 --> 00:08:06,876
we can make some statistical claims
about how much better the agent

123
00:08:06,916 --> 00:08:10,676
with the better tool is than
the agent with just the basic tools.

124
00:08:11,896 --> 00:08:16,996
Alright, but why is linear control a good
idea for contact-rich manipulation?

125
00:08:17,376 --> 00:08:20,516
Isn't gradient famously
bad when you have contact?

126
00:08:21,556 --> 00:08:25,396
So that's a topic that me and my
collaborators have looked into

127
00:08:25,436 --> 00:08:29,496
over the last couple of years,
and we think we have an explanation.

128
00:08:29,536 --> 00:08:36,396
So to make that explanation, let me start
with an even simpler system where it does

129
00:08:36,436 --> 00:08:38,596
have hybrid contact dynamics,

130
00:08:38,636 --> 00:08:41,896
but its dynamics is also so simple
that we can plot in a plane.

131
00:08:42,576 --> 00:08:47,236
So here we have a robot, which is a ball
that can move in the xy plane controlled

132
00:08:47,276 --> 00:08:53,316
by a PD controller, and we have a box
that can slide on a frictional surface.

133
00:08:54,136 --> 00:08:57,616
And the dynamics, in this case,
there is no velocity in the dynamics.

134
00:08:58,076 --> 00:09:03,756
We are simply mapping the current
state and the PD target of the robot

135
00:09:03,796 --> 00:09:08,656
to the next position of the robot,
assuming that everything damps out.

136
00:09:08,796 --> 00:09:13,476
So this is the quasi-dynamic setting
that Mason proposed a long time ago.

137
00:09:13,516 --> 00:09:20,916
And here's what the dynamics looks like
on the horizontal plane and plotting

138
00:09:20,956 --> 00:09:27,476
the action space of the system, and on the
vertical axis and plotting the next state.

139
00:09:28,336 --> 00:09:30,156
So the one feature
that stands out immediately

140
00:09:30,196 --> 00:09:35,436
is that the dynamics consist
of multiple linear affine pieces.

141
00:09:35,476 --> 00:09:43,136
Here we have the sticking
mode in which the robot pushes

142
00:09:43,176 --> 00:09:46,776
down hard enough so
that the robot moves with the box.

143
00:09:47,296 --> 00:09:53,676
And there are two sliding modes
where the object doesn't move as much

144
00:09:53,716 --> 00:09:58,656
as the ball moves, but still does
because there's relative sliding.

145
00:09:59,156 --> 00:10:01,316
And then there is this totally flat mode

146
00:10:01,356 --> 00:10:04,476
where the box is not
touching the ball at all.

147
00:10:05,656 --> 00:10:10,056
And, okay, let's consider the problem
of trying to push the box to the right.

148
00:10:10,956 --> 00:10:15,476
And this problem can be formulated as
an optimal control problem where we try

149
00:10:15,516 --> 00:10:17,876
to minimize the distance
to the goal and the effort

150
00:10:17,916 --> 00:10:21,896
we spend doing that subject
to dynamics constraints.

151
00:10:24,096 --> 00:10:30,796
And I think as a person, as people,
we all intuitively understand that to do

152
00:10:30,836 --> 00:10:33,016
this we need to push down
hard enough to get into

153
00:10:33,056 --> 00:10:36,376
the sticking mode and then drag
the box with our fingertips.

154
00:10:36,996 --> 00:10:39,256
But how does an algorithm figure that out?

155
00:10:39,996 --> 00:10:44,716
Well, an algorithm can figure that out
by inspecting the gradient of the next

156
00:10:44,756 --> 00:10:50,036
state of the box with respect
to the action of all the different pieces.

157
00:10:50,076 --> 00:10:54,016
So that gradient is the largest
in the sticking piece,

158
00:10:54,056 --> 00:10:58,216
which means we should
be in that piece if our goal is to get

159
00:10:58,256 --> 00:11:02,556
to the target position
with the smallest amount of effort.

160
00:11:04,696 --> 00:11:08,756
But if we have a local controller
that starts in a bad position,

161
00:11:08,796 --> 00:11:11,756
so this is the hand grabbing ball example

162
00:11:11,796 --> 00:11:15,516
Professor Todorov just
mentioned a while ago.

163
00:11:16,076 --> 00:11:20,156
If we start here and we use a local
gradient based optimizer to try to find

164
00:11:20,196 --> 00:11:22,996
the best action, we would
be stuck because the gradient

165
00:11:23,036 --> 00:11:25,396
doesn't really tell us
where we should go next.

166
00:11:30,306 --> 00:11:33,246
But that's not how, well,

167
00:11:33,286 --> 00:11:35,706
I think people have been aware
of this problem for a long time.

168
00:11:36,326 --> 00:11:40,366
And even like 10 years ago,
people have figured out solutions,

169
00:11:40,406 --> 00:11:43,186
which is instead of looking at one mode,

170
00:11:43,226 --> 00:11:48,166
we just look at every mode and figure out
how the transition between the modes work.

171
00:11:48,206 --> 00:11:52,886
And you can do things
like pushing a simple

172
00:11:52,926 --> 00:11:56,546
block on the tabletop
with a spherical robot.

173
00:11:58,206 --> 00:12:01,246
So this is Alberto's work
when he was still at MIT.

174
00:12:02,726 --> 00:12:05,486
But the problem is trying
to think about every mode

175
00:12:05,526 --> 00:12:08,686
is that the number of modes
grows very quickly.

176
00:12:09,546 --> 00:12:12,706
Well, not really exponentially, but well,

177
00:12:12,746 --> 00:12:15,186
the polynomial bound doesn't
really help you that much either.

178
00:12:15,226 --> 00:12:21,806
So this is the way we can connect
modes is that we raise the number

179
00:12:21,846 --> 00:12:28,306
of contacts per pair to the number
of collision pairs in the system.

180
00:12:29,346 --> 00:12:33,606
And it's okay for this simple task
because there's only one pair.

181
00:12:34,306 --> 00:12:35,786
But if you look at something
like that with hands,

182
00:12:35,826 --> 00:12:41,406
it's going to be astronomical because,
well, there are just too many pairs.

183
00:12:41,446 --> 00:12:44,506
And I think any efforts
to try to think about

184
00:12:44,546 --> 00:12:47,266
every mode is going
to get stuck very quickly.

185
00:12:48,386 --> 00:12:51,206
And this is even just
reasoning about what's

186
00:12:51,246 --> 00:12:52,966
going to happen over a horizon of one.

187
00:12:53,186 --> 00:12:55,206
We need to multiply this number
by the number of horizon

188
00:12:55,246 --> 00:12:59,526
if we want to have a meaningful
foresight into the future.

189
00:13:00,446 --> 00:13:03,626
But meanwhile, we also
understand that reinforcement

190
00:13:03,666 --> 00:13:06,126
learning is able to power
through this many contact modes.

191
00:13:07,446 --> 00:13:09,726
And I think one of the things we've been,

192
00:13:09,766 --> 00:13:13,446
I and my collaborators
have been doing in our PhDs

193
00:13:13,486 --> 00:13:16,326
is to try to understand how that happened.

194
00:13:17,686 --> 00:13:20,566
So it turns out that eventually
we've attributed the success

195
00:13:20,606 --> 00:13:26,126
of reinforcement learning
to its inherent stochasticity.

196
00:13:27,076 --> 00:13:32,666
So stochasticity is everywhere
in reinforcement learning.

197
00:13:33,166 --> 00:13:39,466
Well, especially policy gradient family of
methods where we sample a bunch of random

198
00:13:39,506 --> 00:13:43,326
actions and we try to figure out
how we should tweak these actions

199
00:13:43,366 --> 00:13:47,346
so that we can collect better
rewards in the next iteration.

200
00:13:48,326 --> 00:13:51,626
And it turns out that this random sampling

201
00:13:51,666 --> 00:13:54,706
of actions has a smoothing
effect on the dynamics.

202
00:13:54,746 --> 00:13:58,646
So for example,
let's reconsider this nominal action

203
00:13:58,686 --> 00:14:03,466
in the separation mode where
it has exactly zero gradient.

204
00:14:04,066 --> 00:14:07,566
But if we sprinkle a bunch
of samples around the nominal action,

205
00:14:07,606 --> 00:14:13,036
we will have some samples in these
modes that have non-zero gradients.

206
00:14:13,906 --> 00:14:17,766
And if we take the average of these
gradients from all these different modes,

207
00:14:17,806 --> 00:14:20,386
we will have a gradient
that's non-zero which will

208
00:14:20,426 --> 00:14:24,706
tell you how you should
change your action.

209
00:14:24,746 --> 00:14:26,066
In order to get to the goal.

210
00:14:27,546 --> 00:14:36,226
And I think one downside of using sampling
to estimate any quantity is the variance.

211
00:14:37,206 --> 00:14:39,646
And the only way to reduce
the variance of your

212
00:14:39,686 --> 00:14:42,446
estimation is to bump up
the number of samples.

213
00:14:42,486 --> 00:14:49,126
So for this randomized smoothing of the
non-smooth landscape of the pusher system,

214
00:14:49,166 --> 00:14:52,806
if we only give the estimator 10 samples,

215
00:14:52,846 --> 00:14:58,086
we would have a pretty jagged
gradient landscape like this one.

216
00:14:58,346 --> 00:15:00,486
But if we bump the number
of samples all the way up to 500,

217
00:15:00,526 --> 00:15:03,886
we have a gradient landscape
that starts to resemble

218
00:15:03,926 --> 00:15:07,326
the gradient landscape
of a smooth dynamical system.

219
00:15:08,846 --> 00:15:13,006
So that's why we think
reinforcement learning

220
00:15:13,046 --> 00:15:16,746
is able to handle
non-smooth contact dynamics.

221
00:15:18,326 --> 00:15:22,506
And it turns out
that this smoothing effect

222
00:15:22,546 --> 00:15:25,746
of sampling on non-smooth
landscapes is not new.

223
00:15:26,066 --> 00:15:28,566
The optimization community
understands this really well.

224
00:15:28,926 --> 00:15:30,926
And what we did here is really
to make the connection between

225
00:15:30,966 --> 00:15:36,786
that smoothing effect and how RL
handles contact dynamics so efficiently.

226
00:15:37,426 --> 00:15:42,666
And here's another cartoon that shows what
happens when we have a dexterous hand.

227
00:15:43,366 --> 00:15:45,246
And this is a cartoon because, well,

228
00:15:45,286 --> 00:15:49,526
the action space
and the state space are higher

229
00:15:49,566 --> 00:15:51,786
dimensional than what I can plot on paper.

230
00:15:52,586 --> 00:15:55,946
So here, each affine piece
is supposed to represent

231
00:15:55,986 --> 00:15:59,146
a particular way in which
the fingers touch the box.

232
00:16:00,166 --> 00:16:05,866
And when RL does sampling, it would have
samples in a bunch of different pieces.

233
00:16:06,486 --> 00:16:08,946
And it would summarize
them into a single linear

234
00:16:08,986 --> 00:16:11,946
piece and then make
progress with that average.

235
00:16:14,006 --> 00:16:17,266
Well, now that we understand
smoothing is a good thing,

236
00:16:17,306 --> 00:16:21,846
we can actually get the same
smoothing effect without any sampling.

237
00:16:21,886 --> 00:16:24,766
This is, again,
the continuation kind of approach

238
00:16:24,806 --> 00:16:27,186
Professor Todorov was
talking about earlier.

239
00:16:28,426 --> 00:16:31,886
So we are solving these
forward dynamics with...

240
00:16:33,186 --> 00:16:36,146
Okay, so almost everyone
formulates contact

241
00:16:36,186 --> 00:16:38,186
dynamics as a convex optimization problem.

242
00:16:38,866 --> 00:16:41,946
And we are solving
it with interior point method.

243
00:16:42,366 --> 00:16:45,466
And we terminate the Newton iterations

244
00:16:45,506 --> 00:16:48,286
early with a fairly
small barrier parameter.

245
00:16:48,326 --> 00:16:52,626
So that all the constraints are kind
of active and acting on the object.

246
00:16:54,046 --> 00:16:56,746
So in some sense, it's even faster
than if you solve it to convergence

247
00:16:56,786 --> 00:17:00,566
and you get the nice smoothing effect
that's going to help you with planning.

248
00:17:03,026 --> 00:17:04,486
Okay, so now that we have a good gradient,

249
00:17:04,526 --> 00:17:07,986
the next thing to figure out
is where this gradient is valid.

250
00:17:08,726 --> 00:17:10,766
Traditionally, people draw a circle around

251
00:17:10,806 --> 00:17:12,926
their nominal action
and adjust its radius.

252
00:17:12,966 --> 00:17:17,726
But I think in non-smooth
gradient landscapes,

253
00:17:17,766 --> 00:17:21,606
drawing a circle is pretty bad
because you can be pretty close

254
00:17:21,646 --> 00:17:26,106
to the boundary and the circle
is going to drop off a cliff sometimes.

255
00:17:27,086 --> 00:17:29,766
And it turns out the nice thing to do
is to recover some of the visibility

256
00:17:29,806 --> 00:17:36,206
constraints in your convex optimization
of contact and linearize them,

257
00:17:36,246 --> 00:17:39,786
add them back so that you have
a more accurate trust region.

258
00:17:41,446 --> 00:17:42,446
Okay,

259
00:17:42,926 --> 00:17:47,086
So now that we know what a good
gradient is and where it's valid,

260
00:17:47,126 --> 00:17:51,706
we can apply this to the problem
we were talking about earlier,

261
00:17:51,746 --> 00:17:53,326
which is to push the box to the right.

262
00:17:54,246 --> 00:17:58,526
So yeah, we start here with initial
guess in the separation mode.

263
00:17:58,566 --> 00:18:04,106
In three iterations,
on the smoothed gradient landscape,

264
00:18:04,146 --> 00:18:06,946
the optimizer is able to figure out
that it should go here and then go

265
00:18:06,986 --> 00:18:10,366
here so that we are in the sticking
mode, pushing the box to the right.

266
00:18:11,506 --> 00:18:16,846
Well, interestingly, the same
simple insight on the planar

267
00:18:16,886 --> 00:18:24,126
system holds up to a variety
of complex dynamical systems.

268
00:18:24,166 --> 00:18:27,546
Here we are showing two
iiwa arms rotating a bucket.

269
00:18:28,086 --> 00:18:31,086
We are showing
this dexterous hand rotating

270
00:18:31,126 --> 00:18:33,466
a box and this is a spot
also turning a box.

271
00:18:33,986 --> 00:18:34,986
Yeah,

272
00:18:35,886 --> 00:18:38,246
I think this is kind
of the beauty of linear control.

273
00:18:38,446 --> 00:18:41,626
When it works in two dimensions, it's
going to work in two hundred dimensions.

274
00:18:43,446 --> 00:18:46,246
Okay, to summarize, I think
contact modes artificially

275
00:18:46,286 --> 00:18:50,106
inflates the hardness of dexterous
manipulation problems.

276
00:18:50,746 --> 00:18:54,026
And what we really should
be doing is to summarize a lot

277
00:18:54,066 --> 00:18:56,726
of these modes into a single
linear piece and use that.

278
00:18:58,226 --> 00:19:02,466
Okay, so better tools
leads to better agent.

279
00:19:02,726 --> 00:19:03,946
What could we do next?

280
00:19:04,846 --> 00:19:07,946
Well, I guess if these
tools work really well,

281
00:19:07,986 --> 00:19:12,726
it's going to get distilled into
the next version of Fable or Astra.

282
00:19:13,186 --> 00:19:16,506
And we keep doing this until there
are no problems left to be solved,

283
00:19:16,546 --> 00:19:17,546
which is a good thing.

284
00:19:17,626 --> 00:19:20,706
I would be flattered if the next
iteration of Fable says,

285
00:19:20,746 --> 00:19:22,346
oh, we should be doing MPC for contact.

286
00:19:23,306 --> 00:19:33,106
But yeah, I think robotics is notoriously
long tail and the importance of these

287
00:19:33,146 --> 00:19:39,526
more efficient methods is not really that
it can solve rotating a body in the hand.

288
00:19:39,566 --> 00:19:43,046
Well, if my boss asked me to show
something like that to an investor

289
00:19:43,086 --> 00:19:47,026
tomorrow, I would probably be asking Fable

290
00:19:47,066 --> 00:19:49,706
to run some PPO overnight
and then show that.

291
00:19:50,546 --> 00:19:54,266
The real importance of these
more efficient methods

292
00:19:54,306 --> 00:19:58,046
is that it can adapt to changes
on the real system more quickly.

293
00:19:58,466 --> 00:20:03,066
There is no way we can pre-train our
models to be able to handle every task.

294
00:20:03,106 --> 00:20:09,186
So speaking of long tail tasks, so this
is a short description of my current job.

295
00:20:10,286 --> 00:20:12,106
Well, the Multiply Labs does cell therapy,

296
00:20:12,146 --> 00:20:15,706
which is personalized
cancer drug discovery,

297
00:20:15,746 --> 00:20:19,806
where we start with extracting
blood from a cancer patient.

298
00:20:19,866 --> 00:20:23,306
We do some genetic engineering
of the cells and then

299
00:20:23,346 --> 00:20:25,606
we inject the cells back
into the patient's body.

300
00:20:25,846 --> 00:20:28,966
Apparently, that's a very
complicated process and takes

301
00:20:29,006 --> 00:20:32,186
a lot of the scientist's
time and energy to do well.

302
00:20:33,106 --> 00:20:35,066
This is one of the tasks they need to do.

303
00:20:35,346 --> 00:20:39,526
So these balls in there are supposed
to represent the clumped

304
00:20:39,566 --> 00:20:43,506
blood cells after you take
them out from cold storage.

305
00:20:44,126 --> 00:20:48,646
And the scientist here is trying
to manually de-clump the cell clumps.

306
00:20:49,966 --> 00:20:55,146
Well, this looks and feels very different
from cooking dinner or folding laundry.

307
00:20:55,646 --> 00:20:57,866
So if Astra is pre-trained
on a lot of household tasks,

308
00:20:57,906 --> 00:21:02,566
it still needs to adapt to some
extent to tasks like these.

309
00:21:03,926 --> 00:21:05,186
So to be truly generalist,

310
00:21:05,226 --> 00:21:09,926
robots need to learn efficiently from
and adapt to real world interactions.

311
00:21:10,146 --> 00:21:13,426
That's where I think these gradient based
tools can be more helpful because they

312
00:21:13,466 --> 00:21:16,646
are just so much more efficient
than just relying on samples

313
00:21:16,686 --> 00:21:19,986
and trying to make progress
slowly with policy gradient.

314
00:21:21,386 --> 00:21:23,126
Okay, so these are my collaborators.

315
00:21:24,326 --> 00:21:26,366
Thank you and happy to take any questions.

316
00:21:35,106 --> 00:21:35,926
Great work.

317
00:21:35,966 --> 00:21:39,946
Do you have any understanding of what your
smoothing dynamics are actually simulated?

318
00:21:40,906 --> 00:21:43,686
Can you run your smoothing model
forward and see what it does?

319
00:21:45,926 --> 00:21:49,766
Oh yes, we actually did that,
at least on simple systems.

320
00:21:50,266 --> 00:21:54,646
It really just looks like I'm
controlling the object with the force.

321
00:21:55,586 --> 00:21:57,086
So force from a distance?

322
00:21:57,126 --> 00:21:57,766
Yes, yes.

323
00:21:57,806 --> 00:21:58,806
Yes,

324
00:22:00,686 --> 00:22:04,226
But what we do in practice
is we fix one parameter,

325
00:22:05,226 --> 00:22:06,006
One smoothing.

326
00:22:06,046 --> 00:22:09,146
Well, we do not really do
continuation in practice.

327
00:22:09,566 --> 00:22:15,346
So we pick a barrier parameter
that is fixed, we compute a gradient,

328
00:22:15,386 --> 00:22:20,666
and then we try to do gradient descent
on error we measured on real systems.

329
00:22:20,706 --> 00:22:24,366
And that turns out
to be working relatively well.

330
00:22:24,806 --> 00:22:28,186
So I think there could be some
theoretic backing to it,

331
00:22:28,226 --> 00:22:30,906
like iterative learning and control.

332
00:22:32,506 --> 00:22:33,506
So

333
00:22:35,336 --> 00:22:38,716
There should be some barrier
parameter you have to tune, right?

334
00:22:38,756 --> 00:22:39,276
Yes.

335
00:22:39,316 --> 00:22:42,876
How sensitive is the barrier?

336
00:22:42,916 --> 00:22:44,336
I see, I see.

337
00:22:44,736 --> 00:22:47,616
I would say it's not very sensitive.

338
00:22:47,956 --> 00:22:50,476
So for all these examples I showed,
so for the 200 goals,

339
00:22:50,516 --> 00:22:55,176
the code that Fable is using,
it's using the same barrier parameter.

340
00:22:56,256 --> 00:22:59,616
And I would say with agents, it's much,

341
00:22:59,656 --> 00:23:02,796
it's a lot easier to tune
these than in the old days.

342
00:23:06,626 --> 00:23:07,626
Any other questions?

